   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 581 of the laws of 2005, amending the local finance law relating to statutory installment bonds, in relation to extending the effectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S3917",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3917-2011",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2, Chap 581 of 2005",
    "lawSection": "Bonds and Notes",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3917\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act\nto amend chapter 581 of the laws of 2005, amending the local finance law\nrelating to statutory installment bonds, in relation to extending the\neffectiveness thereof\n\nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nThe bill would extend for three years, to September 30, 2014, the\nprovisions of Local Finance Law (\"LFL\") § 62.10 in relation to\nstatutory installment bonds issued to the New York State\nEnvironmental Facilities Corporation (\"EFC\").\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill would extend the \"sunset\" provisions of Chapter\n581 of the laws of 2005 through September 30, 2014, thereby continuing\nthe authority of local governments to issue statutory installment\nbonds in an amount not to exceed twenty million dollars to the New\nYork State Environmental Facilities Corporation (\"EFC\") at a fixed\nrate or, if issued in serial maturities, at a set rate for each\nindividual maturity.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would provide an immediate effective date.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nA bond provides written evidence of an issuer's obligation to repay a\nspecified principal amount on a dale certain together with interest\nat a stated rate. Under the LFL, municipalities and public benefit\ncorporation borrowers may issue and sell serial bonds to EFC. A\nseparate bond is issued for each year's maturity and each bond\nindicates the principal and interest amount due on maturity.\n\nLFL § 62.10 allows municipalities to issue a single statutory\ninstallment bond for the full principal amount, not to exceed one\nmillion dollars, if the issue is sold at private sale. Statutory\ninstallment bonds issued and sold to the United States of America or\nany agency thereof may exceed the one million dollar principal\nlimitation.\n\nLFL § 62.10(b) further provides the substantive terms and form the\ninstallment bond is to utilize. This form reflects, among other\nthings, bonds of fixed annual installments of principal and interest.\n\nLFL § 62.10(c) allows municipalities to issue a single statutory\ninstallment bond for the full principal amount, not to exceed twenty\nmillion dollars, to EFC at a fixed rate\n\nor, if issued in serial maturities, at a set rate for each individual\nmaturity. This provision will be deemed repealed as of September 30,\n2011.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nChapter 581 of the Laws of 2005, which would have expired September 30,\n2008, was extended through September 30, 2011 by Chapter 213 of the\nLaws of 2008.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nEFC serves to promote environmental quality by providing low cost\ncapital and expert technical assistance to municipalities, businesses\nand State agencies for environmental projects in New York State. In\nthis role, EFC administers the Clean Water State Revolving Fund\n(CWSRF) and Drinking Water State Revolving Fund (DWSRF).\nUnder these revolving fund programs, EFC offers both leveraged and\ndirect loans.\n\nAs of September 30, 2010, EFC has issued approximately $13.1 billion in\nState Revolving Fund Bonds under the CWSRF and DWSRF to fund loans to\nlocal governments and public benefit corporations.\n\nPrior to 2005, in accordance with the LFL, a borrower receiving an EFC\nfinancing issued serial bonds to EFC for each maturity. For a 20-year\nfinancing, EFC would receive 20 separate bonds from the community.\nThe issuance of serial bonds has caused EFC, and those providing\nTrustee services, excessive servicing cost and substantial time in\nhandling and processing of multiple bonds. For example, for EFC\npooled financings, EFC would receive at loan closing up to 20\nseparate bonds because most loans from the State Revolving Funds are\nfor periods of twenty (20) years. For pooled financings, where 30 to\n50 borrowers may participate at one time, this would require\nreceiving 600 to 1000 separate individual bonds. This process has\nalso resulted in unnecessary bonding costs to borrowing entities.\n\nSerial bonds issued to EFC may reflect a different interest rate for\neach maturity.\nThese interest rates, of course, reflect the pre-established annual\npercentage of principal payable for the use of borrowed funds for a\nparticular maturity. This concept differs from the issuance of bonds\nwith variable rates of interest pursuant to LFL § 54.90. Variable\nrate bonds under section 54.90 allow for the issuance of bonds with\ninterest rates that are subject to adjustment pursuant to a rate\nformula, procedure or index, the computation of the variable rate\nbeing made upon the occurrence of a clearly delineated event.\n\nA single debt instrument for financings at or less than twenty million\ndollars, such as a statutory installment bond, reduces the costs\nassociated with printing, processing and storage. Reduced paper usage\nwill also achieve environmental benefits, while also reducing the\n\nchance of error associated with multiple printed bonds. This\nsimplified approach also benefits borrowers in making the financing\nprocess more \"user friendly\" while enhancing the efficient\npreparation and execution of the bonds.\nAdditional allowances for either a fixed or, if issued in serial\nmaturities, at a set rate of\ninterest for each individual maturity, will enable financing options\nmore reflective of the budgetary realities of the borrowing entity.\n\nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThe bill would have no impact on the State budget.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThe bill would take effect immediately.\n\n",
